After the construction of its railway station, in 1866,[[10]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hengelo#cite_note-10) industrial development accelerated, with a focus on technology: [Stork B.V.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stork_B.V [link_45]. "Stork B.V."), Hazemeyer, [N.V. Heemaf](https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/N.V.%20Heemaf "nl:N.V. Heemaf") (now part of France's [Alstom](link_46), [Brush HMA](link_47), Brush-Barclay, [Eaton Corporation](link_48), [Essent](link_49), Heemaf BV, [Vattenfall](link_50), and [Wabtec](link_51)), KHZ (now [AkzoNobel](link_52)), and [Hollandse Signaal Apparaten](link_53) (now part of the [Thales Group](link_54)). Especially Stork played an important role in the development of Hengelo, building garden village [Tuindorp 't Lansink](https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuindorp_'t_Lansink_(Hengelo) "nl:Tuindorp 't Lansink (Hengelo)") (1910s), with housing for its personnel, while also contributing to building public facilities in the city centre, such as a library.[[11]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hengelo#cite_note-11)

